Most newsreaders, when replying, add an extra > sign at the start of the
line. In the process, some may remove extra spacing between the first
existing > and the extra one added.

The better newsreaders also recognise the | character as a quoting
character, and may treat it the same as the > character, sometimes even
replacing it by the > character. This is normally sound behaviour, but in
the case of a NetHack map, the | character also denotes a left wall.

My own newsreader (outlook express with the OE-QuoteFix addition) also has
this behaviour. For example, the following:

-----
|...|
+...|
|...|
-----

, when shown in my reader, would be replaced by:

-----
> ...|
+...|
> ...|
-----

Subsequent indentation with > might yield an even wierder look.

Things work out allright if there are leading spaces or a wierd quoting
character like any of the following: )}+*.

